// We define an "Anomaly" as any UNEXPECTED problem or condition encountered during program execution.
// PRINCIPLE: 
// - UNEXPECTED (by the program) implies NEITHER manageable NOR recoverable (by the same program).
//   (=> TINA: There Is No Alternative <=)
//   => The ONLY allowed action for a program regarding an anomaly is to RAISE (and TRACK) that
//      anomaly. Then, the program must promptly ABORT execution...
// - TECHNICALLY, raising an anomaly consists entirely of reporting a problem to stderr. From an external
//   perspective, a process that begins writing to stderr must be understood as an unequivocal 
//   announcement of its abortion. Applications must NOT be allowed to pollute stderr and then
//   continue processing as if nothing happened...
//  
// PRACTICAL anomaly tracking: (see also c-ansi/types.topo for the concept of "procedures" and
// "pure functions") 
// The practical technique chosen for tracking anomalies consists of breaking the "normal"
// CONTROL FLOW and returning a "sentinel" value (typically: -1) recursively up to the application's main function. 
// This technique of anomaly escalation is easily implemented within "procedures".
// Escalating anomalies is also possible within "pure functions" with minor adaptations - see
// the '_o' indicator in watermark.topo (NEVER tested...).
